{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789161649,"nonce":"CbtyRXCeArJVe9YqJ8SxePUiKA8=","message":"CORRECTION: the cast is a RED HERRING. The real bug is much broader.\n\n`switch` over an `enum abstract` ALWAYS SELECTS THE FIRST CASE, with no cast\ninvolved anywhere. /tmp/probe/ABC3.hx:\n\n    enum abstract IntK(Int)    { var I0 = 0;  var I1 = 1;  var I2 = 2; }\n    enum abstract SparseK(Int) { var S10 = 10; var S20 = 20; var S30 = 30; }\n    enum abstract StrK(String) { var SA = \"a\"; var SB = \"b\"; var SC = \"c\"; }\n    enum RealEnum { E1; E2; E3; }\n\n    switch (k) { case I0:\"0\"; case I1:\"1\"; case I2:\"2\"; }   etc.\n\n| subject                        | got      | want     |\n|--------------------------------|----------|----------|\n| enum abstract Int   (0,1,2)    | 000      | 012      |\n| enum abstract Int   (10,20,30) | 101010   | 102030   |\n| enum abstract String           | AAA      | ABC      |\n| real `enum`                    | 123      | CORRECT  |\n\nSo it is not `cast this`, and not the underlying type. Every switch over an\nenum abstract matches the first case and stops. A real enum is unaffected.\n\nWhy the original report misread it: `viaThis` used INTEGER LITERAL patterns\n(`case 0:`, `case 1:`) and those work. `viaCast` used enum-abstract NAME\npatterns (`case A:`) and those do not. The discriminator is the PATTERN KIND,\nnot the cast. /tmp/probe/ABC1.hx confirms `cast this` is fine on its own: as\nInt, through a local, and in `==` comparisons, all six forms correct.\n\nImpact is wider than the three Xml rows: this is a silent wrong answer (exit 0)\nfor every enum abstract switch, and `enum abstract` is pervasive in modern\nHaxe and in the stdlib. if/else over the same values is correct, so code that\navoids switch escapes it.","files":null}]}